<article class="content">
<div class="blocks"><div class="row split"><div class="block media"><p><a href="https://astronomycyprus.eu/images/transit2016/Transit2016-05-09Gr.jpg" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><img alt="" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://astronomycyprus.eu/images/transit2016/Transit2016-05-09Gr.jpg" width="600"/></a></p></div><div class="block"><p>Observation of the Transit of Mercury in Cyprus, 9 May 2016</p><p>Thanks to its position, the Moon covers the Sun several times each year, and so solar eclipses occur. But the Moon is not the only celestial body that can lie between our planet and the Sun. Much more rarely, the two "inner" planets, Mercury and Venus, appear to cover part of the Sun after passing in front of it relative to our position on Earth. As for the planet Venus, its last transit took place on 6 June 2012, and the next time observers will enjoy the phenomenon from Earth will be on 10-11 December 2117.</p><p>The situation is a little different with Mercury, the fastest planet. Mercury transits the solar disc about 13 times every 100 years, of which 4 occur in the month of May. During a May transit the planet is closer to the Earth and appears 20% larger, compared with transits that occur in November. Although people began to predict these transits in the Middle Ages, they managed to observe them only after the invention of the telescope. The next transit of Mercury will take place on Monday, 9 May 2016. Throughout the whole year the KITION PLANETARIUM &amp; OBSERVATORY is carrying out the project "Cosmic Light Comes to Cyprus 2016". This extraordinary and rare event is one of the main and most impressive astronomical phenomena that can be observed from the Island of Aphrodite, and it will be widely celebrated. In Cyprus the transit is visible during the afternoon, and the Observatory has prepared its telescopes for the public. On 9 May 2016 the KITION PLANETARIUM &amp; OBSERVATORY is holding a special program for children and adults with a discussion and telescope observations of the transit of Mercury.</p></div></div></div>
<div class="blocks"><div class="row split reverse"><div class="block media"><p><img alt="" decoding="async" height="516" loading="lazy" src="https://astronomycyprus.eu/images/transit2016/tm2016-Fig01bGr.jpg" width="550"/></p></div><div class="block"><p>The first contact of the transit will take place at 14:11:29 and visitors will be able to observe the phenomenon through a large telescope with the appropriate solar filter. The telescope is a Schmidt-Cassegrain catadioptric with an 8-inch optical tube and is perhaps the largest telescope in Cyprus used for public observations on a regular basis. Observers will notice how a notch on the eastern side of the solar disc grows larger and larger, and at 14:14:33 the complete black tiny disc of Mercury will be visible against the background of the solar disc. The angular size of the planet, 12", is 160 times smaller than the angular size of the Sun and will be comparable to the size of sunspots. The size of the planet is too small to be observed with the naked eye. NEVER LOOK AT THE SUN WITH THE NAKED EYE. At 17:57 the transit of Mercury will reach its greatest position. The event will end with sunset at 19:40, since the last phases will not be visible from the KITION PLANETARIUM &amp; OBSERVATORY. The next transit will take place on 11 November 2019 and will again be visible from Cyprus.</p><p>INFORMATION &amp; MANDATORY RESERVATIONS: TEL. +357 99991111</p></div></div></div>
</article>
